{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>You shall not build them of hewn stone.</b> The reason for the prohibition of hewn stone for the altar is that the honor of the altar should not be such that part of the stone is built into the altar of HaShem and part of it is cast away in the rubbish heaps. Thus explained the sage R. E., of blessed memory." "<b>And the Rambam,</b> of blessed memory, wrote a reason for this within the general reasons of the commandments: it is a distancing, lest one come to make a form in them, so that it become a carved stone." "<b>But</b> the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote and gave a reason for this which is the most correct of all that has been said concerning it: because the sword is the strength of Esau, as in the blessing ״על חרבך תחיה״ — 'by your sword you shall live' — and behold, Esau is hated by the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (Malachi 1:3): ״ואת עשו שנאתי״ — 'but Esau I hated' — and behold, he is distanced from the altar. And the hewn stone is not the essence of the prohibition, but rather the lifting of iron, as it is written (Deuteronomy 27:5): ״לא תניף עליהם ברזל״. And if one hewed them with a silver tool or with the shamir, this is permitted by Torah law; but the prohibition is only in order to distance iron from the altar. And Scripture expressed iron in the language of חרב (sword), because it destroys the world; therefore Scripture distances it from the altar, which is the peace of the world and its endurance. And therefore no mention of iron at all is found in all the work of the Tabernacle, only gold and silver and copper. Also in the Temple it is explicitly written (I Kings 6:7): ״ומקבות והגרזן כל כלי ברזל לא נשמע בבית בהבנותו״, and they said in the Sifrei: 'in the house it was not heard, but it was heard outside.'" "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״כי חרבך הנפת עליה״ — from here they said: the altar was created to lengthen a person's days, and iron to shorten a person's days; it is not fitting that the shortener be lifted over the lengthener. And from here you learn that each and every commandment among the commandments of the Torah has many facets, and the words of Torah are like a hammer splitting a rock, dividing into many reasons, and in all of them there is benefit for body and soul, and they are life to those who find them. And so Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 4:22): ״כי חיים הם למוצאיהם ולכל בשרו מרפא״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:01:55.458158697Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}